  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fair most areas today with more seasonable conditions.\n\nNow that the cold front has crossed the region, cooler and drier\nair is arriving from the North/Northwest. There are still some\npre-dawn showers across the Delaware Valley and Delmarva. These\nwill linger into the morning, especially the Delmarva activity,\nthen diminish towards the late afternoon. There is some \nuncertainty with regards to if/when the showers will end across\nsouthern/eastern Delaware today. \n\nHigh pressure will build into the region from the north during Saturday.\nMainly cloudy skies along and southeast of I-95 early will given\nway to clearing skies by afternoon for most of the area. Mostly\ncloudy skies will likely remain the theme all day across Delmarva\nand extreme southern New Jersey though, where the showers may linger\ninto the early afternoon. Highs will be in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers are expected to impact portions of the \nregion Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nLow pressure developing along the stalled frontal boundary to\nour south Sunday and Sunday night will provide another round of\nshowers across portions of the region. The timing of the showers\nwill be focused during the afternoon hours Sunday, but could\nbegin near/south of Philly in the morning and linger area wide\ninto the evening or overnight hours. PoPs have increased some,\nand now range from around 40-50% along and north of I-80 to \naround 60-80% within the I-95 corridor and Philly metro, to \n70-90% across Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. The latest \nQPF forecast ranges from around 0.10\" in the far north, to about\n0.25\" within the I-95 corridor and Philly metro, to 0.50-1\" \nacross Delmarva and far southern New Jersey. It's possible the \nshowers don't make it much farther northwest than the I-95 \ncorridor. There still remains spread in the guidance as to \nexactly how far inland the showers will make it.\n\nGiven overcast skies, easterly winds, and showers, temperatures\nSunday will be cooler with highs likely only reaching the low to\nmid 60s in many areas.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Canadian high pressure will bring much cooler \ntemperatures during the first half of next week, including some\npotential for frost near and north of I-78 Tuesday night.\n\nCanadian high pressure and a much cooler air mass will then \nbuild into the region starting Monday into Tuesday. Monday will\nremain mild with highs within a few degrees of 70, though a\nnorthwest breeze will make it feel cooler. Some lingering clouds\nearly should give way to mostly sunny skies later in the day.\nLows mainly in the 40s to low 50s Monday night.\n\nHigh temperatures Tuesday are only in the low 60s for much of \nthe region, which is upwards of 10 degrees below normal. Tuesday\nwill feature clear and sunny skies though. Some areas of frost \nare possible by Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, especially\nnorth of I-78. Forecast lows are in the mid 30s north of I-78, \nand in the low to mid 40s elsewhere.\n\nAs cold advection wanes by midweek, the air mass will begin to\nmodify and trend notably warmer by Thursday and into Friday. \nHighs will trend closer to normal near 70 degrees.",
